<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
  What this vehicle looks like. Metres and degrees, X forward, Y right, Z up.

    fixture    a part that is seen and does not move, the airframe among them
    propeller  a blade that turns with its motor
    surface    a control surface that deflects

    file   a mesh inside this folder
    xyz    where it sits. For a propeller this is its offset from the motor, which is where its hub
           already is -- and only the part along the turning axis stays put while it spins
    rpy    how it is turned: roll, pitch, yaw
    motor  which engine turns this propeller, by the name Propulsion.xml gives it
    axis   the axis it turns about, in the vehicle's frame; the sign is the direction

  A mesh may be drawn facing any way. Turn it here rather than editing the mesh.
-->
<visual>
  <!-- A quarter turn, and the sign of it was found by looking rather than by working it out: the white
       pair is the front one, modelled at 315 and 45 degrees, and the axis swap glTF applies puts that
       bisector on an axis I twice predicted wrongly. At -90 the red pair led. Nothing is baked into the
       mesh; the arms sit at 45 degrees either way, so the propellers stay on their tips. -->
  <fixture file="meshes/F450_body.glb" xyz="0 0 0" rpy="0 0 -90"/>

  <!-- The hub is the motor's own place, read from Propulsion.xml, so xyz here is only the mesh's offset
       from that hub -- and this blade is modelled on its hub, so it has none.

       Rolled flat. The blade is modelled standing up, and the axes it arrives on are not the ones it was
       drawn with: glTF hands the engine a frame where this mesh's thin dimension -- 11 mm against 262,
       which is the shaft -- lies along Y and the span stands along Z. One roll lays the shaft vertical
       and the disc horizontal. The pair whose <sense> is -1 turns the other way, which is the only
       thing that tells the two pairs apart on screen.

       Only the step up the shaft, and nothing sideways. This offset hangs off the hub, and the hub is
       what turns, so a sideways part would carry the blade around the shaft once per revolution -- which
       is right for x500, whose blade really does sit 146 mm out from its own origin, and wrong here,
       where the mesh is already centred on its hub.

       Sideways there is nothing left to correct. The model and the flight model described two different
       F450s -- the mesh a 450 mm one, motors at 159.0 mm on each axis, and Propulsion.xml a 467 mm one at
       165.1 -- so the blades sat 9 mm outboard of the bells they are bolted to. The mesh is exported 3.8%
       larger and its bells now land on the thrusters within 0.1 mm. Scale is the one thing this project
       bakes into a mesh; the flight model was not touched for the sake of a picture.

       13.8 mm is what remains: the bell top at 38.8 mm against a thruster stated at 25. It is along the
       axis, so it stands still while the blade turns. -->
  <propeller file="meshes/F450_prop.glb" xyz="0 0 0.0138" rpy="90 0 0" motor="front right" axis="0 0 1"/>
  <propeller file="meshes/F450_prop.glb" xyz="0 0 0.0138" rpy="90 0 0" motor="aft left" axis="0 0 1"/>
  <propeller file="meshes/F450_prop.glb" xyz="0 0 0.0138" rpy="90 0 0" motor="front left" axis="0 0 -1"/>
  <propeller file="meshes/F450_prop.glb" xyz="0 0 0.0138" rpy="90 0 0" motor="aft right" axis="0 0 -1"/>
</visual>
